WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.229
Film Courage: Lee you mentioned earlier planning
and tracking software.

00:00:03.229 --> 00:00:07.350
I’m wondering in terms of screenwriting
programs can they really teach someone to

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:08.850
be a better writer?

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:11.190
Lee Jessup: No.

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:12.190
[Laughs].

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:13.329
Truly writing is inspiration.

00:00:13.329 --> 00:00:15.800
Writing is a craft.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:18.980
If you get a hammer, it’s not going to teach
you to be a better carpenter.

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:23.759
It may make the creation of carpentry easier.

00:00:23.759 --> 00:00:26.550
It’s not going to teach you how to be a
better writer.

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:27.550
It is a craft.

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:30.109
It is a craft that has to be practiced.

00:00:30.109 --> 00:00:32.960
Sure there are tools like Dramatica to help
you, etcetera, etcetera.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:36.239
And there is SAVE THE CAT and all of those.

00:00:36.239 --> 00:00:42.920
But I’ve seen writers take on SAVE THE CAT
and not get screenwriting to save their lives.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.640
It’s not paint by numbers.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:49.110
And (you know) if you’re color blind, painting
by numbers is really not going to work for

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:50.670
you and that’s pretty much the equation.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:57.140
So I think that all of those things have their
uses and I think that they can be very important

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:03.370
guides for the mechanics of your work but
you the writer are going to have to master

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:04.370
the craft.

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:08.729
I’m a big believer in Malcolm Gladwell’s
10,000 hours.

00:01:08.729 --> 00:01:11.700
You shouldn’t be shy of getting those.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.810
For most writers, the first script is not
going to be ‘The Script.’

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:16.620
The second script is not going to be ‘The
Script.’

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:20.290
You are going to have to really get better
at your craft and there is no short-cutting

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:21.290
that.

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:28.190
Even if you filled out the 40 beats of SAVE
THE CAT perfectly, that does not mean a perfect

00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:29.400
script, ever.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:36.689
Film Courage: Right…let’s take Malcolm
Gladwell’s THE OUTLIERS in terms of environment

00:01:36.689 --> 00:01:42.740
and fostering genius or genius already being
in someone but environment either hindering

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:46.100
it or getting it out there Have you seen that?

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:48.119
Lee Jessup: Oh certainly!

00:01:48.119 --> 00:01:50.610
Listen there has to be a kernel of genius
there.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:57.500
You can be a technically proficient writer
but if you’re not able to invest some soul

00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:58.500
in your work.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:05.420
If you’re not able to feel the way that
it sings, then it will always feel mechanical.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:10.070
I had a girlfriend growing up who really,
really wanted to be a great pianist.

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:11.570
She never felt it.

00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:18.020
So her playing was entirely proficient but
entirely cold.

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:20.540
And the same thing happens with screenplays.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:26.430
There has to be some (whether we want to call
it genius or anything else) talent, something

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:31.760
innate that you’re able to invest in the
work that makes it uniquely your own or else

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:39.120
it just becomes a perfectly, technically executed
screenplay, but not a great screenplay.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.670
And I do believe that is the difference between
good and great, that extra thing.

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:49.760
And certainly environment can nurture or destroy
it.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:55.030
But I can tell you sadly that I’ve seen
writers come to screenwriting and really,

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:56.690
really want to get it.

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:59.400
But they don’t and you’ve tried every
different way.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:04.730
I was speaking recently to a friend who is
a UCLA instructor who said “I’ve always

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:06.740
believed that anybody can be taught how to
write.

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:09.440
And then every once and awhile somebody walks
into my class that reminds me that…no!

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:11.680
It’s just not the case.”

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:15.650
There are some things that either you understand
them innately or you don’t.

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:21.180
I think most people that come to this come
with some innate understanding of screenwriting.

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.990
With the flow of screenwriting.

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:28.490
With the general structure, the general rhythm
of screenwriting.

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:32.400
But I think you certainly want an environment
that you nurture it, to promote it and to

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:33.690
help you rise to your very best.

